# RegMod slot indexes (enum) vs outside (names)

## Law

- **Inside the owning muscle TU:** fixed export/import tables use
  **module-local enums** as named indexes (`0,1,2,…`). Indexes never leave
  the TU as an API.
- **Outside the module:** resolve only by **string names**
  (`(module, func)` / `find_export` / `RegImport`). Never peer enums,
  never naked export indexes.

## Naming

### Rust

Per-module nested types (not crate-global):

```rust
mod ssh {
    #[repr(usize)]
    pub(crate) enum Export {
        Init,
        Listen,
        Close,
        Release,
        Poll,
        Served,
        ListenPort,
        Status,
        HostkeyLabel,
    }
}
// ssh::Export::Listen — indexes stay in the TU
```

`RegExport` / `RegImport` remain the **slot value types**. Enums are only
indexes. Soft imports (when needed) use a hand-written desc; do not invent
dead imports. `reg_mod!` is optional sugar that hides indexes behind named
statics; hand form should use `Export` / `Import` like C.

### C

Full path prefix (no namespaces):

```c
PM_METAL_<PATH>_EXPORT_<NAME>   /* e.g. PM_METAL_NET_SSH_EXPORT_LISTEN */
PM_METAL_<PATH>_IMPORT_<NAME>   /* e.g. PM_METAL_NET_SSH_IMPORT_YIELD */
```

`<PATH>` mirrors the module path after `pymergetic.metal.` (`net.ssh` →
`NET_SSH`).

